don't know about your life.

00:02:52.842 --> 00:02:54.784
And we talked about this,

00:02:54.784 --> 00:02:57.127
there's some things fathers
and sons don't talk about.

00:02:57.127 --> 00:02:59.416
- You remember when you lived,

00:02:59.416 --> 00:03:01.510
far away from us?

00:03:01.510 --> 00:03:02.634
- Yeah.

00:03:02.634 --> 00:03:03.809
- How long was that?

00:03:03.809 --> 00:03:04.642
For a few years, right?

00:03:04.642 --> 00:03:06.869
- Yeah, about four, four years.

00:03:06.869 --> 00:03:09.054
- Did you ever feel like we didn't get

00:03:09.054 --> 00:03:11.359
to know you very well as kids?

00:03:11.359 --> 00:03:12.859
- Well, sometimes.

00:03:15.118 --> 00:03:18.409
'Cause I didn't spend
much time with you guys.

00:03:18.409 --> 00:03:19.242
- Yeah.

00:03:20.410 --> 00:03:23.602
- I know, I feel like my
memory of this is more extreme,

00:03:23.602 --> 00:03:27.360
but when I was teenager and preteen,

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:30.318
I feel like I was really angry a lot.

00:03:30.318 --> 00:03:34.485
I remember yelling at you
and Mom and especially you.

00:03:35.330 --> 00:03:40.055
And being a kid is frustrating
and scary and angering.

00:03:40.055 --> 00:03:44.222
But, I never thought about how
that must feel as a father.

00:03:48.271 --> 00:03:49.104
- I don't know.

00:03:49.104 --> 00:03:53.431
- Well, that's the hardest
thing about father,

00:03:53.431 --> 00:03:57.264
you try to spend more
time with your children.

00:03:59.297 --> 00:04:01.833
That was, I missed a lot.

00:04:01.833 --> 00:04:04.806
I didn't spend a lot
of time with you guys.

00:04:04.806 --> 00:04:06.433
But you had a good mom.

00:04:06.433 --> 00:04:07.266
- [Eugene] Yeah.

00:04:07.266 --> 00:04:10.364
- She's very tender, caring mom.

00:04:10.364 --> 00:04:13.260
So you guys should appreciate.

00:04:13.260 --> 00:04:16.010
- Well we did, but then she left.

00:04:16.899 --> 00:04:19.066
And then we were with you.

00:04:20.834 --> 00:04:22.125
Right?
- Right.

00:04:22.125 --> 00:04:26.674
- So, what was your early
relationship with Mom like?

00:04:26.674 --> 00:04:29.723
- Well I met her senior year in college.

00:04:29.723 --> 00:04:32.803
And I knew pretty much right away,

00:04:32.803 --> 00:04:35.317
that I wanted to marry
her, but I wasn't ready.

00:04:35.317 --> 00:04:36.150
- Really?

00:04:36.150 --> 00:04:39.247
- Yeah, so we dated and then we broke up,

00:04:39.247 --> 00:04:40.844
and then we dated and we broke up.

00:04:40.844 --> 00:04:41.802
- What?

00:04:41.802 --> 00:04:44.557
- Only that at church, someone came to me,

00:04:44.557 --> 00:04:46.258
said, "Hey Don, we think you

00:04:46.258 --> 00:04:48.602
"should help teach Sunday school."

00:04:48.602 --> 00:04:49.602
I said okay.

00:04:50.950 --> 00:04:51.940
- Is this where Mom was?

00:04:51.940 --> 00:04:54.851
- And Mom was already kinda
in charge of the youth group,

00:04:54.851 --> 00:04:57.283
and she was really good with the youth,

00:04:57.283 --> 00:05:00.445
and really pretty, and really smart,

00:05:00.445 --> 00:05:02.936
and we seemed to, we compatible

00:05:02.936 --> 00:05:05.022
and she had lots of
strengths where I didn't,

00:05:05.022 --> 00:05:05.855
and I had some strengths

00:05:05.855 --> 00:05:07.176
where she might not have had,

00:05:07.176 --> 00:05:10.416
and together it seemed like
it was gonna work out well.

00:05:10.416 --> 00:05:12.496
One of the first movies we went to,

00:05:12.496 --> 00:05:15.167
was when Star Wars first came out.

00:05:15.167 --> 00:05:17.343
Very first time, we were
both blown away equally.

00:05:17.343 --> 00:05:18.176
- You both liked it?

00:05:18.176 --> 00:05:19.109
- Yeah.
- That's a good sign.

00:05:19.109 --> 00:05:19.942
- Yeah.

00:05:21.058 --> 00:05:25.785
- Yeah, I had no idea that you
broke up with my mom before.

00:05:25.785 --> 00:05:27.645
- Well I was an idiot.

00:05:27.645 --> 00:05:29.159
I was immature and I was an idiot.

00:05:29.159 --> 00:05:30.476
- Obviously.

00:05:30.476 --> 00:05:35.117
- I thought I needed to date
other people for a while.

00:05:35.117 --> 00:05:38.609
And I did, and it just
wasn't, wasn't the same.

00:05:38.609 --> 00:05:41.218
She just had a really great sense of humor

00:05:41.218 --> 00:05:43.135
that I appreciated and,

00:05:45.486 --> 00:05:47.337
the things that I appreciated her,

00:05:47.337 --> 00:05:50.534
with her in the beginning,
are the same now,

00:05:50.534 --> 00:05:51.862
but there's so many other things

00:05:51.862 --> 00:05:53.493
that I appreciate about her.

00:05:53.493 --> 00:05:55.660
That actually became more,

00:05:58.125 --> 00:06:00.042
more intense with kids.

00:06:01.142 --> 00:06:02.576
And having children actually has

00:06:02.576 --> 00:06:04.772
made our relationship stronger.

00:06:04.772 --> 00:06:07.081
- Then maybe, I think
that I got to know you

00:06:07.081 --> 00:06:08.706
better after you divorced, right?

00:06:08.706 --> 00:06:09.539
- Right.

00:06:21.928 --> 00:06:25.928
- So, when your dad died,
when you were how old?

00:06:28.051 --> 00:06:31.359
- I was about three months old.

00:06:31.359 --> 00:06:32.776
- How did he die?

00:06:33.827 --> 00:06:36.607
- Well, he died with a stroke.

00:06:36.607 --> 00:06:37.440
- He had a stroke?

00:06:37.440 --> 00:06:38.909
- Mm-hmm.

00:06:38.909 --> 00:06:40.746
- And then, when did your mom die?

00:06:40.746 --> 00:06:43.443
- Well, she left us, so.

00:06:43.443 --> 00:06:44.276
- Oh.

00:06:44.276 --> 00:06:47.206
- Yeah, we didn't have any communication.

00:06:47.206 --> 00:06:48.039
